# .gitattributes

# Line endings are declared HERE rather than left to each developer's `core.autocrlf`, which is a
# machine setting and not part of the repository. Checked 2026-08-19: the two rules below covered 9 of
# 2261 tracked files, and `*.sh` matched none — so normalisation was resting entirely on one local git
# config being set the same way on every clone. The index happened to be clean (every file stored as LF,
# none mixed), but 28 files already carried MIXED endings in the WORKING TREE, and CI runs on Linux where
# `core.autocrlf` defaults to false. A rule that only holds because one machine is configured a
# particular way is not a rule.
#
# This line only declares the intent. Files already committed keep their stored form until somebody runs
# `git add --renormalize .`, which rewrites whatever differs and is a separate, deliberate commit.
*  text=auto


*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.sh text eol=lf